00:18:50,000 --> 00:18:56,000
And that was in 1992.

367
00:18:56,000 --> 00:19:00,000
And the National Stock Exchange was all electronic.

368
00:19:00,000 --> 00:19:02,000
And so, it was the modern version.

369
00:19:02,000 --> 00:19:06,000
It's rapidly gaining on the Bombay Stock Exchange.

370
00:19:07,000 --> 00:19:12,000
So, let me go a little bit more forward.

371
00:19:12,000 --> 00:19:16,000
China, because of the communist government, did

372
00:19:16,000 --> 00:19:20,000
not have a stock exchange until 1990.

373
00:19:20,000 --> 00:19:27,000
And there were two stock exchanges founded

374
00:19:27,000 --> 00:19:34,000
in China in 1990, Shanghai and Shenzhen.

375
00:19:34,000 --> 00:19:35,000
And at least the Shanghai Stock

376
00:19:35,000 --> 00:19:36,000
Exchange is owned by the Chinese.

377
00:19:36,000 --> 00:19:40,000
And that's why Laura Cha, when she talked to

378
00:19:40,000 --> 00:19:44,000
us about that, she was on the China Securities

379
00:19:44,000 --> 00:19:46,500
Regulatory Commission, which actually

380
00:19:46,500 --> 00:19:49,000
owned the Shanghai Stock Exchange.

381
00:19:49,000 --> 00:19:57,000
So, that's the – oh, Latin America.

382
00:19:57,000 --> 00:20:04,000
Sao Paulo Stock Exchange, 1890.

383
00:20:06,000 --> 00:20:09,000
Mexico has only one stock exchange,

384
00:20:09,000 --> 00:20:12,000
but it was founded in 1894.

385
00:20:12,000 --> 00:20:13,500
So, we seem to have

386
00:20:13,500 --> 00:20:15,000
like two different kinds of exchanges.

387
00:20:15,000 --> 00:20:18,500
We have the old exchanges, at least 100

388
00:20:18,500 --> 00:20:22,000
years old, and we have the new electronic exchanges.

389
00:20:22,000 --> 00:20:24,500
Things have really sped up

390
00:20:24,500 --> 00:20:27,000
with the advent of electronic exchanges.

391
00:20:27,000 --> 00:20:30,500
So, I mentioned the New York Stock Exchange, which started

392
00:20:30,500 --> 00:20:34,000
on the street, outdoors, and is an old-fashioned exchange that –

393
00:20:34,000 --> 00:20:35,000
– that's the New York Stock Exchange.

394
00:20:35,000 --> 00:20:38,000
It's a very old-fashioned exchange

395
00:20:38,000 --> 00:20:41,000
that has been slow to update.

396
00:20:41,000 --> 00:20:46,500
But more recently, we have –

397
00:20:46,500 --> 00:20:52,000
I mentioned it already – NASDAQ.

398
00:20:52,000 --> 00:20:57,500
That's the – it stands for originally

399
00:20:57,500 --> 00:21:03,000
National Association of Securities Dealers Automatic Quotation System,

400
00:21:03,000 --> 00:21:04,000
which was created in the 1970s.

401
00:21:05,000 --> 00:21:10,000
The interesting story about NASDAQ, in the 70s,

402
00:21:10,000 --> 00:21:15,000
the New York Stock Exchange was highly prestigious.

403
00:21:15,000 --> 00:21:19,000
It was the big board, the place.

404
00:21:19,000 --> 00:21:23,000
And a critical element of a stock exchange is that

405
00:21:23,000 --> 00:21:27,000
in order to get your stocks traded on the exchange,

406
00:21:27,000 --> 00:21:30,000
you have to satisfy listing requirements.

407
00:21:30,000 --> 00:21:31,500
So, the New York Stock Exchange would

408
00:21:31,500 --> 00:21:33,000
examine any corporation that wanted to be –

409
00:21:33,000 --> 00:21:36,000
listed on the exchange.

410
00:21:36,000 --> 00:21:39,000
And it was the prestigious, big exchange.

411
00:21:39,000 --> 00:21:41,000
So, it had high standards.

412
00:21:41,000 --> 00:21:44,000
So, the company had to have a history of earnings.

413
00:21:44,000 --> 00:21:47,000
It had to have the right

414
00:21:47,000 --> 00:21:50,000
kind of management structure and board.

415
00:21:50,000 --> 00:21:53,000
A lot of things were checked out by the exchange.

416
00:21:53,000 --> 00:21:57,000
And as a result, the way it would work in the

417
00:21:57,000 --> 00:22:01,000
1970s, a startup company could never get traded on the exchange.

418
00:22:01,000 --> 00:22:04,500
It would be traded instead by

419
00:22:04,500 --> 00:22:08,000
brokers off exchange or over the counter.

420
00:22:08,000 --> 00:22:13,000
So, OTC means over the counter.

421
00:22:13,000 --> 00:22:16,000
That means not on an exchange.

422
00:22:16,000 --> 00:22:22,500
So, in the 1970s and earlier, the over-the-counter brokers would

423
00:22:22,500 --> 00:22:29,000
deal with each other informally with the New York Stock Exchange.

424
00:22:29,000 --> 00:22:32,000
Or actually, out on the street.

425
00:22:32,000 --> 00:22:34,500
They would meet each other out on

426
00:22:34,500 --> 00:22:37,000
the sidewalk originally, and then they got telephones.

427
00:22:37,000 --> 00:22:41,500
And they had some record, which they called pink

428
00:22:41,500 --> 00:22:46,000
sheets, because they were traditionally printed on pink paper.

429
00:22:46,000 --> 00:22:50,500
These were lists of dealers buy and

430
00:22:50,500 --> 00:22:55,000
sell quotes on prices of over-the-counter stocks.

431
00:22:55,000 --> 00:22:57,000
The National Association of Securities

432
00:22:57,000 --> 00:22:59,000
deals with the stock market.

433
00:22:59,000 --> 00:23:01,500
And the dealers then was

434
00:23:01,500 --> 00:23:04,000
an organization of these over-the-counter traders.

435
00:23:04,000 --> 00:23:08,000
And in the early 70s, they

436
00:23:08,000 --> 00:23:12,000
set up the first computerized system.

437
00:23:12,000 --> 00:23:15,000
They decided that everyone's telephoning everybody.

438
00:23:15,000 --> 00:23:21,500
Let's create a system that really

439
00:23:21,500 --> 00:23:28,000
works and that gets us the information.

440
00:23:29,000 --> 00:23:33,000
And so, that was the NASDAQ system.

441
00:23:33,000 --> 00:23:36,500
The first computer-based system, which has now

442
00:23:36,500 --> 00:23:40,000
increasingly taken over much of the world.

443
00:23:40,000 --> 00:23:42,500
I shouldn't imply that New York Stock

444
00:23:42,500 --> 00:23:45,000
Exchange was entirely a laggard on this.

445
00:23:45,000 --> 00:23:50,000
Electronics played a role in stocks going back very early.

446
00:23:50,000 --> 00:23:53,000
The New York Stock Exchange used telegraph

447
00:23:53,000 --> 00:23:56,000
in the 19th century to convey prices.

448
00:23:56,000 --> 00:23:58,000
And they invented ticker tape machines.

449
00:23:58,000 --> 00:24:01,000
A ticker tape machine is an electronic

450
00:24:01,000 --> 00:24:04,000
printer that would print out stock prices.

451
00:24:04,000 --> 00:24:07,500
And in fact, Thomas Edison, the inventor, his

452
00:24:07,500 --> 00:24:11,000
first invention was actually a ticker tape machine.

453
00:24:11,000 --> 00:24:13,000
That was in, I think,

454
00:24:13,000 --> 00:24:15,000
the 1870s that printed stock prices.

455
00:24:15,000 --> 00:24:17,000
But all it was, was a

456
00:24:17,000 --> 00:24:19,000
record of what had traded recently.

457
00:24:19,000 --> 00:24:24,000
It wasn't a system that helped you trade.

458
00:24:24,000 --> 00:24:26,000
It just reported what had happened.

459
00:24:26,000 --> 00:24:27,000
It was historical.

460
00:24:28,000 --> 00:24:30,500
So, I wanted to show you

461
00:24:30,500 --> 00:24:33,000
what NASDAQ created in the 70s.

462
00:24:33,000 --> 00:24:37,000
And it's an order book that would

463
00:24:37,000 --> 00:24:41,000
be visible to everyone who trades on it.

464
00:24:41,000 --> 00:24:48,500
So, prior to discussing that, I want

465
00:24:48,500 --> 00:24:56,000
to tell you about different kinds of orders.

466
00:24:58,000 --> 00:25:01,000
If you buy and sell stock, and you call up

467
00:25:01,000 --> 00:25:04,000
a broker, and you say, I want to buy and sell,

468
00:25:04,000 --> 00:25:08,000
the simplest kind of order is a market order.

469
00:25:12,000 --> 00:25:15,000
And you would specify the quantity.

470
00:25:15,000 --> 00:25:16,500
You would say, I want to buy,

471
00:25:16,500 --> 00:25:18,000
and the name of the company, of course.

472
00:25:18,000 --> 00:25:20,500
I want to buy 100 shares of General

473
00:25:20,500 --> 00:25:23,000
Motors, or I want to sell 100 shares.

474
00:25:23,000 --> 00:25:26,000
But you don't name a price.

475
00:25:26,000 --> 00:25:29,000
You'll find out whatever the price was.

476
00:25:29,000 --> 00:25:32,000
The broker will get you the best price.

477
00:25:32,000 --> 00:25:34,500
If he or she is a good broker,

478
00:25:34,500 --> 00:25:37,000
we'll try to get you the best price.

479
00:25:37,000 --> 00:25:40,000
But it'll still be unknown to

480
00:25:40,000 --> 00:25:43,000
you, because you didn't specify it.

481
00:25:43,000 --> 00:25:45,000
You might be unhappy with the price.

482
00:25:45,000 --> 00:25:48,000
The price might be too high.

483
00:25:48,000 --> 00:25:50,000
And then the broker will say to you,

484
00:25:50,000 --> 00:25:52,000
well, if you're unhappy, you should have told me.

485
00:25:52,000 --> 00:25:53,000
You could have told me not

486
00:25:53,000 --> 00:25:54,000
to pay more than a certain amount.

487
00:25:54,000 --> 00:25:54,500
You could have told me not

488
00:25:54,500 --> 00:25:55,000
to pay more than a certain amount.

489
00:25:56,000 --> 00:25:58,000
For a buy.

490
00:25:58,000 --> 00:25:59,500
Or not to take less than

491
00:25:59,500 --> 00:26:01,000
a certain amount for a sell.

492
00:26:01,000 --> 00:26:05,000
So, the alternative is a limit order.

493
00:26:07,000 --> 00:26:10,000
And so, with a limit order,

494
00:26:10,000 --> 00:26:13,000
you give both quantity and price.

495
00:26:15,000 --> 00:26:19,000
So, if it's a buy, I want to buy so many shares,

496
00:26:19,000 --> 00:26:23,000
but I don't want to pay more than such and such a price.

497
00:26:23,000 --> 00:26:26,000
Then the broker will keep that on its mind.

498
00:26:26,000 --> 00:26:31,000
And then you can ask for the price on his or her

499
00:26:31,000 --> 00:26:36,000
books until, well, whatever the agreement between you and the broker is.

500
00:26:36,000 --> 00:26:38,000
It might expire after the day is over.

501
00:26:38,000 --> 00:26:40,000
Or you could ask to

502
00:26:40,000 --> 00:26:42,000
have it kept on the book.

503
00:26:42,000 --> 00:26:47,500
And when the price becomes available, which is no higher

504
00:26:47,500 --> 00:26:53,000
than your specified price, then the order will be executed.

505
00:26:53,000 --> 00:26:55,000
Otherwise, it won't be executed.

506
00:26:55,000 --> 00:26:58,000
And you specify both a quantity and a price,

507
00:26:58,000 --> 00:27:01,000
and the order will be filled or partly filled.

508
00:27:01,000 --> 00:27:05,000
You might not be able to get all of your quantity, but they'll

509
00:27:05,000 --> 00:27:09,000
fill as much as they can of it at that price or lower.

510
00:27:09,000 --> 00:27:16,000
And there's another kind of order called a stop order.

511
00:27:16,000 --> 00:27:23,000
With a stop order, you also specify quantity.

512
00:27:23,000 --> 00:27:28,000
You also specify quantity and price.

513
00:27:28,000 --> 00:27:29,000
But it's different.

514
00:27:29,000 --> 00:27:33,000
With a limit order, you would

515
00:27:33,000 --> 00:27:37,000
buy, say it's a buy limit order.

516
00:27:37,000 --> 00:27:39,000
Well, let's talk about sell.

517
00:27:39,000 --> 00:27:42,500
If it's a sell limit order, you would sell

518
00:27:42,500 --> 00:27:46,000
the quantity at such and such a price or higher.

519
00:27:46,000 --> 00:27:49,000
With a stop order, you would sell that

520
00:27:49,000 --> 00:27:52,000
quantity at such and such a price or lower.

521
00:27:52,000 --> 00:27:54,000
I have to make that clear.

522
00:27:54,000 --> 00:28:00,000
A stop order, also called a stop loss order, is an order that you can

523
00:28:00,000 --> 00:28:06,000
place with the broker to indicate that I'm worried that this stock might really collapse.

524
00:28:06,000 --> 00:28:09,000
I'm holding it, but I want you to

525
00:28:09,000 --> 00:28:12,000
sell it if the price starts falling a lot.

526
00:28:12,000 --> 00:28:15,000
So, suppose the price is 100 today.

527
00:28:15,000 --> 00:28:18,000
I could put a stop loss order at 80.

528
00:28:18,000 --> 00:28:19,500
And then, at least I know I

529
00:28:19,500 --> 00:28:21,000
can't lose more than 20% of my investment.

530
00:28:21,000 --> 00:28:25,000
Because the broker will immediately sell it when

531
00:28:25,000 --> 00:28:29,000
the price of that stock falls below 80.

532
00:28:29,000 --> 00:28:31,000
There's also a buy stop order.

533
00:28:31,000 --> 00:28:34,000
And that would be something that someone would

534
00:28:34,000 --> 00:28:37,000
rationally do if that person had shorted a stock.

535
00:28:37,000 --> 00:28:39,500
So, if you had shorted a stock, and you were

536
00:28:39,500 --> 00:28:42,000
worried that the price would go up and ruin you,

537
00:28:42,000 --> 00:28:46,500
you can leave with your broker a buy stop order

538
00:28:46,500 --> 00:28:51,000
to buy the stock whenever the price exceeds a certain amount.

539
00:28:51,000 --> 00:28:54,000
And you would do that to prevent yourself

540
00:28:54,000 --> 00:28:57,000
from having unlimited losses on your short position.

541
00:28:57,000 --> 00:28:59,000
There's other kinds of orders, but

