for over 150 years without any substantive change.

804
00:42:48,000 --> 00:42:50,500
But now, electronic trading is coming

805
00:42:50,500 --> 00:42:53,000
in, and everything is being shaken up.

806
00:42:53,000 --> 00:42:56,500
So, the New York Stock

807
00:42:56,500 --> 00:43:00,000
Exchange merged with Archipelago in 2005.

808
00:43:00,000 --> 00:43:06,000
And then, they did another merger, let's

809
00:43:06,000 --> 00:43:12,000
say, the New York Stock Exchange with Euronext,

810
00:43:12,000 --> 00:43:19,000
which is another exchange in Europe, in 2006.

811
00:43:19,000 --> 00:43:22,500
And right now, they're going

812
00:43:22,500 --> 00:43:26,000
through a merger, another merger process.

813
00:43:26,000 --> 00:43:28,000
Apparently, with the Deutsche Bank.

814
00:43:28,000 --> 00:43:35,000
And that's 2011.

815
00:43:35,000 --> 00:43:37,000
It's not finalized yet.

816
00:43:37,000 --> 00:43:40,000
And now, NASDAQ is making an

817
00:43:40,000 --> 00:43:43,000
offer for the New York Stock Exchange.

818
00:43:43,000 --> 00:43:45,000
And so, is another

819
00:43:45,000 --> 00:43:47,000
exchange called the Intercontinental Exchange.

820
00:43:47,000 --> 00:43:49,500
But the little guys are

821
00:43:49,500 --> 00:43:52,000
buying up the old-time big guys.

822
00:43:52,000 --> 00:43:56,000
Laura Cha was saying in her lecture, she said,

823
00:43:56,000 --> 00:43:58,500
that we used to think

824
00:43:58,500 --> 00:44:01,000
of stock exchanges as like utilities.

825
00:44:01,000 --> 00:44:03,000
Each country has its own stock exchange.

826
00:44:03,000 --> 00:44:05,000
It's the pride of each country.

827
00:44:05,000 --> 00:44:07,000
But now, it's not happening anymore.

828
00:44:07,000 --> 00:44:10,000
And this reflects a bigger and broader trend,

829
00:44:10,000 --> 00:44:11,500
that economies are becoming more

830
00:44:11,500 --> 00:44:13,000
and more integrated across the world.

831
00:44:13,000 --> 00:44:17,000
So, the idea that there will be a

832
00:44:17,000 --> 00:44:21,000
stock exchange for each country is becoming dated.

833
00:44:21,000 --> 00:44:22,500
So, the New York Stock Exchange

834
00:44:22,500 --> 00:44:24,000
is a new kind of stock exchange.

835
00:44:24,000 --> 00:44:26,500
So, the New York Stock Exchange

836
00:44:26,500 --> 00:44:29,000
may soon be a German company.

837
00:44:29,000 --> 00:44:35,000
But that's what happens in modern times.

838
00:44:35,000 --> 00:44:43,000
So, now, I wanted to talk

839
00:44:43,000 --> 00:44:51,000
about some problems with high frequency trading.

840
00:44:51,000 --> 00:44:53,000
As things get so electronic,

841
00:44:53,000 --> 00:44:58,000
let me give you one example.

842
00:44:58,000 --> 00:45:06,000
In 1987, this was the early days of electronic trading.

843
00:45:06,000 --> 00:45:09,000
But still, it had advanced pretty far.

844
00:45:09,000 --> 00:45:14,000
On October 19th of 1987,

845
00:45:14,000 --> 00:45:17,500
the stock markets in the United

846
00:45:17,500 --> 00:45:21,000
States fell, according to the S&P 500,

847
00:45:21,000 --> 00:45:23,000
over 20% in one month.

848
00:45:23,000 --> 00:45:26,000
In one day.

849
00:45:26,000 --> 00:45:30,000
The government did a study.

850
00:45:30,000 --> 00:45:35,000
President Reagan ordered a study

851
00:45:35,000 --> 00:45:39,000
and put it in charge of Nicholas Brady.

852
00:45:39,000 --> 00:45:41,500
And so, the so-called Brady Commission did

853
00:45:41,500 --> 00:45:44,000
a report on why the stock market,

854
00:45:44,000 --> 00:45:47,000
that was the biggest single

855
00:45:47,000 --> 00:45:50,000
stock market drop in U.S. history.

856
00:45:50,000 --> 00:45:52,000
And the Brady Commission did a report

857
00:45:52,000 --> 00:45:54,000
on that stock market drop.

858
00:45:54,000 --> 00:45:58,000
And concluded that program trading, computer trading,

859
00:45:58,000 --> 00:46:02,000
had played a big role in the drop.

860
00:46:02,000 --> 00:46:06,000
There had been a development of programs

861
00:46:06,000 --> 00:46:08,000
that were called portfolio insurance,

862
00:46:08,000 --> 00:46:12,000
sell strategies.

863
00:46:12,000 --> 00:46:14,000
They called it portfolio insurance.

864
00:46:14,000 --> 00:46:16,000
But it wasn't really insurance.

865
00:46:16,000 --> 00:46:19,000
It was an automatic sell strategy.

866
00:46:19,000 --> 00:46:20,000
It's like a stop-loss order,

867
00:46:20,000 --> 00:46:21,000
but a more sophisticated one.

868
00:46:21,000 --> 00:46:28,000
That could be executed in continuous time by a program.

869
00:46:28,000 --> 00:46:33,000
And that led to an instability in the market

870
00:46:33,000 --> 00:46:38,000
that was not anticipated and shocked the world.

871
00:46:38,000 --> 00:46:42,000
So, the Brady Commission made a number of recommendations.

872
00:46:42,000 --> 00:46:46,500
Notably, the Commission recommended that

873
00:46:46,500 --> 00:46:51,000
the exchanges impose trading halts.

874
00:46:51,000 --> 00:46:58,000
That would prevent stocks from dropping,

875
00:46:58,000 --> 00:47:01,000
the whole market from crashing.

876
00:47:01,000 --> 00:47:04,000
So, the New York Stock Exchange and other exchanges,

877
00:47:04,000 --> 00:47:07,000
after the Brady Commission report,

878
00:47:07,000 --> 00:47:13,000
instituted what are called circuit breakers.

879
00:47:13,000 --> 00:47:16,000
And these are automatic market halts

880
00:47:16,000 --> 00:47:18,000
that stop the market when prices are falling,

881
00:47:18,000 --> 00:47:19,000
to help prevent another 1987,

882
00:47:19,000 --> 00:47:23,000
type stock market crash.

883
00:47:23,000 --> 00:47:31,000
So, now, the system is getting complicated.

884
00:47:31,000 --> 00:47:34,000
Even before this,

885
00:47:34,000 --> 00:47:37,000
the United States government had created,

886
00:47:37,000 --> 00:47:40,000
had passed a set of rules

887
00:47:40,000 --> 00:47:43,000
in response to complaints

888
00:47:43,000 --> 00:47:48,000
about people not being given a share of the stock.

889
00:47:49,000 --> 00:47:52,000
And they were not given the best price.

890
00:47:52,000 --> 00:47:54,000
So, here's the problem.

891
00:47:54,000 --> 00:47:58,000
We have multiple exchanges.

892
00:47:58,000 --> 00:48:02,000
New York Stock Exchange is one of many.

893
00:48:02,000 --> 00:48:05,000
And if you call a stock broker,

894
00:48:05,000 --> 00:48:07,000
the stock broker doesn't,

895
00:48:07,000 --> 00:48:10,000
has discretion over where,

896
00:48:10,000 --> 00:48:14,000
which exchange the broker will use to fill your order.

897
00:48:14,000 --> 00:48:17,000
And so, the broker might choose an exchange

898
00:48:17,000 --> 00:48:19,000
that doesn't give you the best price.

899
00:48:19,000 --> 00:48:22,000
And the broker can, in effect,

900
00:48:22,000 --> 00:48:25,000
rip you off as a broker.

901
00:48:25,000 --> 00:48:27,000
In fact, there's a practice

902
00:48:27,000 --> 00:48:30,000
called the payment for order flow.

903
00:48:35,000 --> 00:48:39,000
So, a stock broker,

904
00:48:39,000 --> 00:48:42,000
who is receiving orders from retail clients,

905
00:48:42,000 --> 00:48:45,000
may find that there is a dealer

906
00:48:45,000 --> 00:48:48,000
that's willing to pay for order flow.

907
00:48:48,000 --> 00:48:51,000
When a customer asks to buy the stock,

908
00:48:51,000 --> 00:48:54,000
don't put it through the New York Stock Exchange.

909
00:48:54,000 --> 00:48:55,000
Give it to me,

910
00:48:55,000 --> 00:48:57,000
and I'll give you, the broker,

911
00:48:57,000 --> 00:49:00,000
a fee for directing the order my way.

912
00:49:00,000 --> 00:49:05,000
And that may not serve the client well,

913
00:49:05,000 --> 00:49:07,000
because the client then

914
00:49:07,000 --> 00:49:09,000
might end up paying a higher price.

915
00:49:09,000 --> 00:49:12,000
So, there were a lot of complaints about this.

916
00:49:12,000 --> 00:49:14,000
And it's a difficult problem,

917
00:49:14,000 --> 00:49:17,000
because it's hard to monitor everything that people do.

918
00:49:17,000 --> 00:49:19,000
And there might be justifications

919
00:49:19,000 --> 00:49:21,000
for a payment for order flow.

920
00:49:21,000 --> 00:49:24,000
But there have been efforts

921
00:49:24,000 --> 00:49:26,000
to try to make it a fairer system.

922
00:49:26,000 --> 00:49:30,000
And in 1975,

923
00:49:30,000 --> 00:49:32,000
the U.S. Congress set up something called

924
00:49:32,000 --> 00:49:34,000
the National Market System.

925
00:49:34,000 --> 00:49:40,000
And, oops, I think,

926
00:49:40,000 --> 00:49:46,000
so,

927
00:49:47,000 --> 00:49:51,000
the NMS is the National Market System,

928
00:49:51,000 --> 00:49:57,000
and the ITS is the Intermarket Trading System.

929
00:49:57,000 --> 00:50:00,000
What the government in the United States did,

930
00:50:00,000 --> 00:50:02,000
is it said that brokers

931
00:50:02,000 --> 00:50:04,000
have to get the best price,

932
00:50:04,000 --> 00:50:07,000
or it's called best bid, best offer,

933
00:50:07,000 --> 00:50:12,000
best bid for their clients.

934
00:50:12,000 --> 00:50:15,000
And they have a responsibility for their clients

935
00:50:15,000 --> 00:50:23,000
to take the market with the best price.

936
00:50:23,000 --> 00:50:27,000
So, in conjunction with this,

937
00:50:27,000 --> 00:50:30,000
the exchanges built something called

938
00:50:30,000 --> 00:50:34,000
the Consolidated Quotation System

939
00:50:34,000 --> 00:50:40,000
that allows brokers to see prices on various exchanges

940
00:50:40,000 --> 00:50:43,000
and direct the order of the client

941
00:50:43,000 --> 00:50:45,000
to the exchange that's showing up

942
00:50:45,000 --> 00:50:49,000
during the best price.

943
00:50:49,000 --> 00:50:52,000
So, that is a system that was started

944
00:50:52,000 --> 00:50:54,000
in the United States in 1975.

945
00:50:54,000 --> 00:50:57,000
And brokers still have an obligation

946
00:50:57,000 --> 00:50:59,000
to get the best price for their customers,

947
00:50:59,000 --> 00:51:02,000
regardless of exchange.

948
00:51:02,000 --> 00:51:04,000
But the obligation is hard for the government

949
00:51:04,000 --> 00:51:10,000
to monitor and it gets complicated.

950
00:51:10,000 --> 00:51:13,000
For example, if you're confronting this system,

951
00:51:13,000 --> 00:51:15,000
and your broker wants

952
00:51:15,000 --> 00:51:18,000
500 shares, well, I can't fill them all

953
00:51:18,000 --> 00:51:21,000
at the same price, right?

954
00:51:21,000 --> 00:51:25,000
Well, actually, I could here.

955
00:51:25,000 --> 00:51:28,000
If the broker wanted 10,000 shares,

956
00:51:28,000 --> 00:51:30,000
they'd be all different prices,

957
00:51:30,000 --> 00:51:33,000
because I have all these different customers, right?

958
00:51:33,000 --> 00:51:35,000
I'm asking different amounts.

959
00:51:35,000 --> 00:51:38,000
So, what do you want us to do, SEC?

960
00:51:38,000 --> 00:51:41,000
And so, the SEC recently clarified this,

961
00:51:41,000 --> 00:51:43,000
I think it was in 2006.

962
00:51:43,000 --> 00:51:45,000
Well, we only mean the amount

963
00:51:45,000 --> 00:51:48,000
that you have to hit this, the best one.

964
00:51:48,000 --> 00:51:51,000
There's one up here, 2400 for 2524.

965
00:51:51,000 --> 00:51:54,000
How you fill the others, we can't get into that.

966
00:51:54,000 --> 00:51:58,000
So, it's not a complete protection for customers,

967
00:51:58,000 --> 00:52:00,000
but there still is this obligation

968
00:52:00,000 --> 00:52:05,000
for brokers to use the national market system

969
00:52:05,000 --> 00:52:07,000
to get the best price.

970
00:52:07,000 --> 00:52:11,000
But the system is complicated and confusing,

971
00:52:11,000 --> 00:52:13,000
because there's so many computers involved,

972
00:52:13,000 --> 00:52:14,000
there's so many different exchanges,

973
00:52:14,000 --> 00:52:16,000
and there's so many rules.

974
00:52:16,000 --> 00:52:21,000
It's hard for people to keep up with it.

975
00:52:21,000 --> 00:52:28,000
So, I had on the reading

976
00:52:28,000 --> 00:52:35,000
list, I had a report that I,

977
00:52:35,000 --> 00:52:36,000
well, I found the report.

978
00:52:36,000 --> 00:52:39,000
It was very much in the news a while back.

979
00:52:39,000 --> 00:52:43,000
This is a report on May 6, 2010.

980
00:52:43,000 --> 00:52:49,000
You remember what happened then?

981
00:52:49,000 --> 00:52:51,000
That was not that long ago.

982
00:52:51,000 --> 00:52:56,000
The stock market, as of around 2.30 in the afternoon

983
00:52:56,000 --> 00:53:00,000
in the United States, had fallen 4%.

984
00:53:00,000 --> 00:53:04,000
And then, within a matter of minutes,

985
00:53:04,000 --> 00:53:07,000
it dropped another 6%.

986
00:53:07,000 --> 00:53:09,000
And then, it rebounded quickly.

987
00:53:09,000 --> 00:53:12,000
Some individual stocks dropped practically

988
00:53:12,000 --> 00:53:14,000
to nothing.

989
00:53:14,000 --> 00:53:15,500
And you could buy, you know,

990
00:53:15,500 --> 00:53:17,000
a $30 stock for 30 cents

991
00:53:17,000 --> 00:53:19,000
or something like that.

992
00:53:19,000 --> 00:53:21,000
And then, they rebounded.

993
00:53:21,000 --> 00:53:24,000
So, what happened?

994
00:53:24,000 --> 00:53:27,500
Why did we have this very

995
00:53:27,500 --> 00:53:31,000
brief crash in the stock markets?

996
00:53:31,000 --> 00:53:33,000
It wasn't like 1987, where the market went down

997
00:53:33,000 --> 00:53:36,000
and stayed down.

998
00:53:36,000 --> 00:53:40,000
If you look at closing prices, nothing much happened.

999
00:53:40,000 --> 00:53:41,000
It was this brief.

1000
00:53:41,000 --> 00:53:43,000
A glitch, which probably cost

1001
00:53:43,000 --> 00:53:45,000
some people huge amounts of money.

1002
00:53:45,000 --> 00:53:47,000
Because if you were trading right at that moment,

1003
00:53:47,000 --> 00:53:49,000
you'd have a problem.

1004
00:53:49,000 --> 00:53:50,000
So, I have as an

1005
00:53:50,000 --> 00:53:51,000
optional reading on the reading list,

1006
00:53:51,000 --> 00:53:56,000
a study that was made of May 6, 2010,

1007
00:53:56,000 --> 00:54:00,000
by the SEC and the Commodity Futures Trading Commission,

1008
00:54:00,000 --> 00:54:05,000
trying to understand what happened then.

1009
00:54:05,000 --> 00:54:10,000
And the study does focus on high-frequency trading.

1010
00:54:10,000 --> 00:54:14,000
There were a lot of computers trading automatically

1011
00:54:14,000 --> 00:54:16,000
at that moment in time.

1012
00:54:16,000 --> 00:54:18,000
So, what apparently happened on May 6 is,

1013
00:54:18,000 --> 00:54:22,000
the market was already in a stressed mode

1014
00:54:22,000 --> 00:54:24,000
before 2.30 p.m.

1015
00:54:24,000 --> 00:54:26,000
The VIX index had shot up.

1016
00:54:26,000 --> 00:54:27,000
There was some bad news.

1017
00:54:27,000 --> 00:54:29,000
The market was down 4%.

1018
00:54:29,000 --> 00:54:31,000
There was some bad news.

1019
00:54:31,000 --> 00:54:33,000
So, that meant that some traders were wondering,

1020
00:54:33,000 --> 00:54:34,000
what's going on?

1021
00:54:34,000 --> 00:54:38,000
And maybe they decided to drop out for a while

1022
00:54:38,000 --> 00:54:40,000
and just be cautious.

1023
00:54:40,000 --> 00:54:43,000
But the computers were still trading.

1024
00:54:43,000 --> 00:54:45,000
And then something happened.

1025
00:54:45,000 --> 00:54:47,000
The computers started trading

1026
00:54:47,000 --> 00:54:49,000
back and forth in milliseconds.

1027
00:54:49,000 --> 00:54:50,500
And I don't know what

1028
00:54:50,500 --> 00:54:52,000
the programs were supposed to do,

1029
00:54:52,000 --> 00:54:56,000
or what they, maybe nobody knows the whole picture.

1030
00:54:56,000 --> 00:54:58,000
But the volume of trade

1031
00:54:58,000 --> 00:55:00,000
just went to an astronomical level.

1032
00:55:00,000 --> 00:55:03,000
And it scared people off.

1033
00:55:03,000 --> 00:55:05,000
And so, there were a lot

1034
00:55:05,000 --> 00:55:07,000
of trading pauses that were put.

1035
00:55:07,000 --> 00:55:08,000
Exchanges have rules about that.

1036
00:55:08,000 --> 00:55:09,000
And individual dealerships have rules about that.

1037
00:55:10,000 --> 00:55:12,000
And dealers will say, I'm dropping out.

1038
00:55:12,000 --> 00:55:13,000
I see all this volume.

1039
00:55:13,000 --> 00:55:15,000
I'm not in here anymore.

1040
00:55:15,000 --> 00:55:18,000
So, it remains that the trading that was left

1041
00:55:18,000 --> 00:55:21,000
was substantially computer trading.

1042
00:55:21,000 --> 00:55:27,000
And the market became very illiquid.

1043
00:55:27,000 --> 00:55:34,000
And so, this study has recommended fixes for this.

1044
00:55:34,000 --> 00:55:38,000
But it doesn't recommend ending high-frequency trading.

1045
00:55:38,000 --> 00:55:40,000
A lot of people would recommend doing that.

1046
00:55:40,000 --> 00:55:43,500
There's a popular anger, especially since

1047
00:55:43,500 --> 00:55:47,000
this May 6, 2010 crisis occurred

1048
00:55:47,000 --> 00:55:50,000
during the period of financial crisis.

1049
00:55:50,000 --> 00:55:53,000
And people kind of imagine that the two are linked.

1050
00:55:53,000 --> 00:55:55,000
I think they're kind of independent.

1051
00:55:55,000 --> 00:55:58,500
I think the May 6, 2010 phenomenon

1052
00:55:58,500 --> 00:56:02,000
was due to some kind of anomalies

1053
00:56:02,000 --> 00:56:06,000
or infamiliarity with high-frequency trading.

1054
00:56:06,000 --> 00:56:08,000
It's a glitch.

1055
00:56:08,000 --> 00:56:11,000
And not a major fault.

1056
00:56:11,000 --> 00:56:13,000
But it led to a

1057
00:56:13,000 --> 00:56:15,000
lot of anger about high-frequency trading.

1058
00:56:15,000 --> 00:56:17,000
But I've talked to some

1059
00:56:17,000 --> 00:56:19,000
people at the Chicago Mercantile Exchange

1060
00:56:19,000 --> 00:56:23,000
and others who think that the

1061
00:56:23,000 --> 00:56:27,000
public anxiety over high-frequency trading is misplaced.

1062
00:56:27,000 --> 00:56:28,000
It's kind of inevitable.

1063
00:56:28,000 --> 00:56:30,000
The future is computers, right?

1064
00:56:30,000 --> 00:56:32,000
They're replacing people all over.

1065
00:56:32,000 --> 00:56:34,000
Not in a judgment thing.

1066
00:56:34,000 --> 00:56:35,500
You know, someone was saying at

1067
00:56:35,500 --> 00:56:37,000
the CME meeting where I was,

1068
00:56:37,000 --> 00:56:40,000
that the basic business that we're doing is

1069
00:56:40,000 --> 00:56:43,000
still the same as it was 100 years ago.

1070
00:56:43,000 --> 00:56:45,000
But now we have laptops, right?

1071
00:56:45,000 --> 00:56:47,000
It's just like when you write a term paper.

1072
00:56:47,000 --> 00:56:49,000
It's basically the same thing that somebody

1073
00:56:49,000 --> 00:56:51,000
would have done with a feather pen

1074
